# Define an Agent

This tutorial builds an invoice Agent with the DSL, creates an instance, runs one Turn on it, and then builds the same definition from a Map. No process starts at any point.

## Install Jido

```elixir
Mix.install([
  {:jido, github: "agentjido/jido", branch: "migration/consolidated-v3"}
])
```

## Write the Action

A route needs something to run. This Action adds a payment to the current state. It reads that state from the `:agent_state` context key and returns the complete next state.

```elixir
defmodule DefineGuide.ApplyPayment do
  use Jido.Action,
    name: "define_guide_apply_payment",
    schema: Jido.Joi.object(%{amount_cents: Jido.Joi.integer() |> Jido.Joi.min(1)})

  @impl Jido.Action
  def run(%{amount_cents: amount}, %{agent_state: state}) do
    {:ok, %{state | paid_cents: state.paid_cents + amount}}
  end
end
```

## Define the Agent

`use Jido.Agent` compiles the DSL into a validated definition. An invalid definition fails compilation.

```elixir
defmodule DefineGuide.InvoiceAgent do
  use Jido.Agent,
    name: "invoice",
    description: "Tracks payments for one invoice"

  state do
    schema Jido.Joi.object(%{
             total_cents: Jido.Joi.integer(),
             paid_cents: Jido.Joi.integer() |> Jido.Joi.default(0)
           })
  end

  routes do
    signal_source "/billing/invoice"

    route "invoice.payment.apply", DefineGuide.ApplyPayment, as: :apply_payment
  end
end
```

`use Jido.Agent` accepts these options:

| Option | Meaning | Default |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `:name` | Agent name. Required. | none |
| `:description` | Display text. | `nil` |
| `:id` | Stable Catalog ID. This is separate from an instance ID. | The module name |
| `:vsn` | Positive definition version. | `1` |
| `:metadata` | Static definition data. | `%{}` |
| `:plugins` | Ordered Plugin modules or `{Plugin, config}` tuples. | `[]` |

The `state`, `routes`, and `topology` sections are optional. Each can occur once.

## Read the definition

A definition is a `Jido.Agent` struct with `nil` for both `id` and `state`. The same struct type holds an instance.

```elixir
definition = DefineGuide.InvoiceAgent.definition()

true = Jido.Agent.definition?(definition)
{definition.id, definition.state, definition.vsn}
```

The cell returns `{nil, nil, 1}`.

## Create an instance

An instance adds a nonempty string `id` and complete `state`. The schema fills in `paid_cents` from its default.

```elixir
invoice = DefineGuide.InvoiceAgent.instantiate!(id: "inv-1001", state: %{total_cents: 5_000})

true = Jido.Agent.instance?(invoice)
invoice.state
```

The cell returns `%{paid_cents: 0, total_cents: 5000}`.

`instantiate/1` accepts only `:id` and `:state`. It returns an error when a value does not satisfy the schema, so a missing `total_cents` stops creation here:

```elixir
{:error, %Jido.Error.ValidationError{code: code, path: path}} =
  DefineGuide.InvoiceAgent.instantiate(id: "inv-1002")

{code, path}
```

The cell returns `{:invalid_state, [:state]}`.

## Run one Turn

The `as: :apply_payment` route option generated `apply_payment_signal/2` and `apply_payment_signal!/2`. They build an input Signal with the route's type and the Agent's `signal_source`. They do not validate or run the target.

```elixir
{:ok, signal} = DefineGuide.InvoiceAgent.apply_payment_signal(%{amount_cents: 1_500})
{signal.type, signal.source}
```

The cell returns `{"invoice.payment.apply", "/billing/invoice"}`.

`cmd/3` runs one Turn and returns the next Agent value with a list of Directives. The source value does not change, and nothing has been saved or dispatched.

```elixir
{:ok, paid, []} = DefineGuide.InvoiceAgent.cmd(invoice, signal)

{invoice.state.paid_cents, paid.state.paid_cents}
```

The cell returns `{0, 1500}`.

## Build the same definition from a Map

`Jido.Agent.define!/1` validates a plain Map and returns the same value that the DSL lowers to. The `:module` field records the owning module, so it is set here to make the two values comparable.

```elixir
from_map =
  Jido.Agent.define!(%{
    module: DefineGuide.InvoiceAgent,
    name: "invoice",
    description: "Tracks payments for one invoice",
    schema:
      Jido.Joi.object(%{
        total_cents: Jido.Joi.integer(),
        paid_cents: Jido.Joi.integer() |> Jido.Joi.default(0)
      }),
    signal_source: "/billing/invoice",
    routes: [
      %{
        path: "invoice.payment.apply",
        handler: %{kind: :action, target: DefineGuide.ApplyPayment},
        as: :apply_payment
      }
    ]
  })

from_map === definition
```

The cell returns `true`.

A definition without a module has no generated functions. Use the `Jido.Agent` functions directly, and build the Signal with `Jido.Signal.new!/3`.

```elixir
standalone = %{from_map | module: nil} |> Map.from_struct() |> Jido.Agent.define!()

instance = Jido.Agent.instantiate!(standalone, id: "inv-1003", state: %{total_cents: 900})

payment = Jido.Signal.new!("invoice.payment.apply", %{amount_cents: 900}, source: "/billing/api")
{:ok, settled, []} = Jido.Agent.cmd(instance, payment)

settled.state.paid_cents
```

The cell returns `900`.

## Functions the DSL generates

| Function | Result |
| --- | --- |
| `definition/0` | The validated definition |
| `instantiate/1`, `instantiate!/1` | One validated instance |
| `cmd/3` | One Turn, through `Jido.Agent.cmd/3` |
| `schema/0` | The complete state schema, including Plugin fields |
| `vsn/0` | The definition version |
| `route_executable!/1` | The Action or Flow target for one exact route path |
| `<as>_signal/2`, `<as>_signal!/2` | An input Signal for a route with `:as` and an exact path |

